@page
@model TheQult.Pages.IndexModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "";
}

<section class="hero">
    <h1>Hospitality is a gift.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
        TheQult is a free hospitality community: people hosting people, no money involved.
        It exists because the internet used to work this way — and we think it still can.
    </p>
    <p>
        <a class="button" asp-page="/Join">Join the community</a>
        <a class="button secondary" asp-page="/Members">Find hosts</a>
    </p>

    <form class="search-form" method="get" asp-page="/Members">
        <div>
            <label for="city">Where are you going?</label>
            <input type="text" id="city" name="city" placeholder="Rome, Lisbon, Buenos Aires…" />
        </div>
        <div>
            <label for="hosting">Who should I meet?</label>
            <select id="hosting" name="hosting">
                <option value="">Anyone</option>
                <option value="host">People who can host</option>
                <option value="maybe">People who might</option>
            </select>
        </div>
        <div>
            <button type="submit" class="button">Search hosts</button>
        </div>
    </form>
</section>

<div class="stats">
    <div class="stat"><b>@Model.Members</b><span>members</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>@Model.Hosts</b><span>can host tonight</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>@Model.References</b><span>references written</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>€0</b><span>membership fee, forever</span></div>
</div>

<section class="prose section">
    <h2>What happened to Couchsurfing</h2>
    <p>
        Once, Couchsurfing was a free community: people who hosted people,
        gratuitously. You wrote to a stranger on the other side of the world,
        they lent you their couch, and for one night the world was a place
        where people trusted each other.
    </p>
    <p>
        Then, after Covid, came the trick. A couple of clicks into the sign-up
        flow and you found yourself paying <strong>€65</strong> for a
        “lifetime” subscription. It wasn't for life: today they ask
        <strong>€10 a month</strong> — to host people for free. A community
        built by its members, for free, was sold back to them one payment
        at a time.
    </p>
    <p>
        TheQult exists to bring back that spirit of generosity the internet
        once had. Hosting is not a product here, and it never will be.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="section">
    <h2>The rules, few and clear</h2>
    <ul class="principles">
        <li>
            <b>Free, forever.</b>
            <p>No subscriptions, no premium tier, no paid “verification badge”.
            The only cost is the server, and it costs less than a coffee a month.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>Hospitality is a gift.</b>
            <p>Not a service: no rooms, no commercial reviews. Whoever hosts
            owes nothing to anyone.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>Trust is earned, not bought.</b>
            <p>After an accepted stay, host and guest write each other a
            reference. It is the only score there is here.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <b>No ads, no data for sale.</b>
            <p>A profile shows what hospitality needs: a name, a city, a hosting
            status, and the references you earned. Nothing more, never sold.</p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</section>
